<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jagat Seth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jagat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a>, meaning 'banker of the world', was the hereditary title of a Marwari banking family based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, granted by the Mughal emperor in 1723. The family managed the Bengal mint and revenue remittances and lent to nawabs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> alike. Its head Mahtab Chand joined the plot with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a> that led to the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a> at Plassey in 1757.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jagat-seth.jpg" alt="Jagat Seth" width="320" height="143" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gangulybiswarup · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:House_Of_Jagat_Seth_Complex_-_Mahimapur_-_Murshidabad_2017-03-28_6165-6169.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
